  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 229 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nTuesday night into Wednesday, upper-level ridging will become \nestablished across the region, while the strongest positive 500-mb \nheight anomalies remain centered near Hudson Bay and a weak mid-\nlevel trough persists across the eastern United States. Strong \nsurface high pressure across the northeastern United States will \nmaintain cooler low-level flow across the region Tuesday night, with \nlows generally in the upper 40s to lower 50s. By Wednesday \nafternoon, a shortwave embedded within the southern-stream flow is \nforecast to lift northeastward toward the region. Southeasterly low-\nlevel flow will also become established, allowing moisture transport \nto increase through the day. Precipitation chances should increase \nas a result, particularly Wednesday evening into the overnight \nhours. Deep-layer moisture transport appears increasingly favorable, \nwith precipitable water values forecast to reach 150 to 200 percent \nof normal and near the 95th to 99th percentile for this time of \nyear. A relatively strong 250-300 mb southern-stream jet streak will \nalso approach the region, providing additional upper-level support \nand large-scale ascent. The combination of anomalous moisture and \nincreasing forcing could support a more widespread area of \nprecipitation Wednesday night into Thursday. This period may \nrepresent the next best opportunity for widespread wetting rainfall, \nparticularly across southwest and western Nebraska. The anomalously \nmoist environment may also support locally heavier rainfall rates, \nthough uncertainty remains regarding the placement of the heaviest \nprecipitation. Trends will continue to be monitored over the next \nseveral forecast cycles. Highs Wednesday should remain in the upper \n60s to lower 70s as increasing cloud cover limits daytime heating.\n\nThursday, southwesterly flow aloft associated with the southern-\nstream system will remain established across the region. Continued \nfavorable moisture transport and large-scale ascent should maintain \nprecipitation chances through at least a portion of the day. \nAnomalously high precipitable water values will also remain in \nplace, supporting efficient rainfall processes and the potential for \nlocally heavier rainfall. Depending on the speed of the system, \nperiods of precipitation could persist across portions of the area \nbefore gradually shifting eastward. Highs Thursday will remain in \nthe upper 60s to lower 70s, with considerable cloud cover once again \nlimiting daytime heating.\n\nFriday into the weekend, upper-level ridging is forecast to rebuild \nacross the region as the southern-stream system departs to the east. \nIncreasing subsidence and gradually decreasing moisture should \nresult in precipitation chances diminishing from west to east. The \nridge does not appear particularly amplified at this time, so a \nsignificant warmup is not expected. Temperatures should remain \nseasonable to slightly cool, with highs generally in the 60s and 70s \nthrough the weekend.",
